es.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{\\frac{4x^{2}}{y^{2}}} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{2^{2}x^{2}}{y^{2}}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot x \\cdot x}{y \\cdot y}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\cdot x \\cdot 2 \\cdot x}{y \\cdot y}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{2x}{y} \\cdot \\frac{2x}{y}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(\\frac{2x}{y})^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{(\\frac{2x}{y})^{2}} &= \\mid \\frac{2x}{y} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}2x &}{\\mid y\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & \\cdot & x &}{\\mid y\\end{vmatrix}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2\\begin{vmatrix}x &}{\\mid y\\end{vmatrix}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186063968 1074 "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that $4, x^2,$ and $y^2$ are all perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot x}{y\\cdot y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\cdot x\\cdot 2\\cdot x}{y\\cdot y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{2x}{y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2x}{y})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2x}{y})}^{2}} & =|\\frac{2x}{y}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|2x|}{|y|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2|\\cdot |x|}{|y|} \\\\  & =\\frac{2|x|}{|y|}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186064122 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{16y^2}$." 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186064122 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{16y^2}.$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186064122 1074 "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that $16$ and $y^2$ are both perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{16y^{2}} &= \\sqrt{4^{2} \\cdot y^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 \\cdot 4 \\cdot y \\cdot y} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 \\cdot y \\cdot 4 \\cdot y} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4y \\cdot 4y} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(4y)^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{(4y)^{2}} &= \\mid 4y \\mid \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}4 & \\cdot & y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 4 \\mid y \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186064122 1074 "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that $16$ and $y^2$ are both perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{16{y}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 4\\cdot y\\cdot y} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot y\\cdot 4\\cdot y} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4y\\cdot 4y} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(4y)^{2}} & =|4y| \\\\  & =|4|\\cdot |y| \\\\  & =4|y|\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186064141 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{36a^2c^2}$." 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186064141 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{36a^2c^2}.$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186064141 1074 "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that $36, a^2$, and $c^2$ are perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{36a^{2}c^{2}} &= \\sqrt{6^{2} \\cdot a^{2} \\cdot c^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{6 \\cdot 6 \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot c \\cdot c} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{6 \\cdot a \\cdot c \\cdot 6 \\cdot a \\cdot c} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(6ac) \\cdot (6ac)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(6ac)^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{(6ac)^{2}} &= \\mid 6ac \\mid \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}6 & \\cdot & ac\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6 \\mid ac \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186064141 1074 "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that $36, a^2,$ and $c^2$ are perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{36{a}^{2}{c}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}\\cdot {a}^{2}\\cdot {c}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6\\cdot 6\\cdot a\\cdot a\\cdot c\\cdot c} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6\\cdot a\\cdot c\\cdot 6\\cdot a\\cdot c} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(6ac)\\cdot (6ac)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(6ac)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(6ac)^{2}} & =|6ac| \\\\  & =|6|\\cdot |ac| \\\\  & =6|ac|\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186064279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481671 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186064305 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481666 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186064305 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481673 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186064330 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481663 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186064461 1050 420.0 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186064494 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186064494 1071 "$\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{2}}{81{q}^{2}}}=$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186064494 1071 "What is $\\sqrt{\\frac{p^{2}}{81q^{2}}}$?" 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186064494 1074 "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that ${p}^{2},81,$ and ${q}^{2}$ are all perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{2}}{81{q}^{2}}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{2}}{{9}^{2}\\cdot {q}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{p\\cdot p}{9\\cdot 9\\cdot q\\cdot q}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{p\\cdot p}{9\\cdot q\\cdot 9\\cdot q}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{p}{9q}\\cdot \\frac{p}{9q}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{p}{9q})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{p}{9q})}^{2}} & =|\\frac{p}{9q}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|p|}{|9q|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|p|}{|9|\\cdot |q|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|p|}{9|q|}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186064700 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186064700 1071 "$\\sqrt{9{a}^{2}}=$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186064700 1071 "What is $\\sqrt{9a^{2}}$?" 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186064700 1074 "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that $9$ and ${a}^{2}$ are both perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9{a}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}\\cdot {a}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3\\cdot 3\\cdot a\\cdot a} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3\\cdot a\\cdot 3\\cdot a} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3a\\cdot 3a} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3a)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(3a)^{2}} & =|3a| \\\\  & =|3|\\cdot |a| \\\\  & =3|a|\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186065343 1064 "When simplifying an algebraic expression involving square roots, it's useful to remember that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nIt's tempting to think that $\\sqrt{x^2}$ is equivalent to $x$, but that's not true because $\\sqrt{x^2}$ is always non-negative, even if $x$ is negative.\n\nTo illustrate, let's evaluate $\\sqrt{x^2}$ for a couple of different values of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{4^{2}} &= \\sqrt{16} = 4 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{16} = 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn both cases, using absolute value gives the correct result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{4^{2}} = & 4 & &= 4 & ✓ \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}} = & -4 & |= 4 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186065343 1064 "When simplifying an algebraic expression involving square roots, it's useful to remember that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|.\n$$\n\nIt's tempting to think that $\\sqrt{x^2}$ is equivalent to $x,$ but that's not true because $\\sqrt{x^2}$ is always non-negative, even if $x$ is negative.\n\nTo illustrate, let's evaluate $\\sqrt{x^2}$ for a couple of different values of $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{16}=4 \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{16}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn both cases, using absolute value gives the correct result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}}=|4| & =4 & ✓ \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}}=|-4| & =4 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186128991 1071 "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{R}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A$, where\n\n![](images/60/606ce428283013cc4e3e5969b634d5536a61f8512c98a627a582816c7de02338.svg)\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186128991 1071 "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{R}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A$, whe