an}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{8}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+6\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186134260 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186134260 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S = Span{a_{1}, a_{2}}$ where\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${a_{1}, a_{2}}$ is orthogonal." 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186134260 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186134260 1074 "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}{a}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-2)^{2}+(1-2)^{2}+(3-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186134269 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186134269 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S = Span{a_{1}, a_{2}}$, where\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${a_{1}, a_{2}}$ is orthogonal." 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186134269 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186134269 1074 "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{4\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{1}=(1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{4\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{7}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{7}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+7[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(4-\\frac{10}{3})}^{2}+{(1-\\frac{7}{3})}^{2}+{(2-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{16}{9}+\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186134300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131839 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186134300 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\},$ where\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal." 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186134300 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w},$ where\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal." 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186134300 1074 "Since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{v\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{w\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}w=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{2}{3}{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{18}\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{6\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186134300 1074 "Since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{v}$ and $\\text{Span}{w}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}w=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{2}{3}{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{18}\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{6\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186135087 1064 "Let's now prove the formula for the orthogonal projection of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspaces $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1 \\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ given that $\\mathbf{a}_1\\perp \\mathbf{a}_2.$ \n\nWe start by writing $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of two vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}_S + \\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_S=\\text{proj}_S\\,\\mathbf{x}$ is in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\},$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp}$ is in the orthogonal complement of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$. That is, $\\mathbf{x}_S=k_1\\mathbf{a}_1+k_2\\mathbf{a}_2\\in S\\,$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\in S^\\perp.$\n\nFrom the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =x-{x}_{S} \\\\  & =x-({k}_{1}{a}_{1}+{k}_{2}{a}_{2}) \\\\  & =x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp}$ is orthogonal to $S,$ and the fact that $\\mathbf{a}_1\\cdot\\mathbf{a}_2=0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{2}=0\\end{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}(x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2})\\cdot {a}_{1}=0 \\\\ (x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2})\\cdot {a}_{2}=0\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {a}_{1}-{k}_{1}({a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1})-{k}_{2}({a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{1})=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {a}_{2}-{k}_{1}({a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{2})-{k}_{2}({a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2})=0\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}({a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1})=x\\cdot {a}_{1} \\\\ {k}_{2}({a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2})=x\\cdot {a}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}=\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}} \\\\ {k}_{2}=\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain our formula \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={k}_{1}{a}_{1}+{k}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}+\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186135087 1064 "Let's now prove the formula for the orthogonal projection of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspaces $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1 \\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ given that $\\mathbf{a}_1\\perp \\mathbf{a}_2.$ \n\nWe start by writing $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of two vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}_S + \\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_S=\\text{proj}_S\\,\\mathbf{x}$ is in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\},$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp}$ is in the orthogonal complement of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$. That is, $\\mathbf{x}_S=k_1\\mathbf{a}_1+k_2\\mathbf{a}_2\\in S\\,$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\in S^\\perp.$\n\nFrom the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =x-{x}_{S} \\\\  & =x-({k}_{1}{a}_{1}+{k}_{2}{a}_{2}) \\\\  & =x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp}$ is orthogonal to $S,$ and the fact that $\\mathbf{a}_1\\cdot\\mathbf{a}_2=0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{2}=0\\end{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,\\,{\\begin{aligned}(x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2})\\cdot {a}_{1}=0 \\\\ (x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2})\\cdot {a}_{2}=0\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,\\,{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {a}_{1}-{k}_{1}({a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1})-{k}_{2}({a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{1})=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {a}_{2}-{k}_{1}({a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{2})-{k}_{2}({a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2})=0\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,\\,{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}({a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1})=x\\cdot {a}_{1} \\\\ {k}_{2}({a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2})=x\\cdot {a}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,\\,{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}=\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}} \\\\ {k}_{2}=\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain our formula \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={k}_{1}{a}_{1}+{k}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}+\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186135088 1064 "Let $n,m$ be positive integers such that $n\\leq m.$ Given an orthogonal set $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\big\\} \\subset  \\Bbb R^m$ and a vector $\\mathbf x \\in \\Bbb R^m,$ we define the **distance between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the vector space $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,  \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$** as\n\n$$\n\\Vert{ \\color{purple} \\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} }\\Vert.\n$$\n\nWe can also define the **angle between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$** to be the acute angle $\\theta$ between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and its orthogonal projection $\\text{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x},$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/07/07fa0d011bbfd49f89ddc5035a27839640a0c2624046d39fbfe8f57c476b5b25.png)\n\n \n\nFinally, notice that we can represent the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of two orthogonal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\underbrace{\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\mathbf{x}}_{\\large \\in S} + \\underbrace{(\\mathbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\mathbf{x})}_{\\large \\in S^\\perp}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Note:** The vector $\\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x},$ whose norm $\\| \\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \\|$ represents the distance from $\\mathbf{x}$ to $S,$ is sometimes called the **vector rejection of $\\mathbf{x}$ from $S.$**" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186135088 1064 "Let $n,m$ be positive integers such that $n\\leq m.$ Given an orthogonal set $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\big\\} \\subset  \\Bbb R^m$ and a vector $\\mathbf x \\in \\Bbb R^m,$ we define the **distance between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the vector space $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,  \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$** as\n\n$$\n\\Vert{ \\color{purple} \\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} }\\Vert.\n$$\n\nWe can also define the **angle between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$** to be the acute angle $\\theta$ between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and its orthogonal projection $\\text{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x},$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/07/07fa0d011bbfd49f89ddc5035a27839640a0c2624046d39fbfe8f57c476b5b25.png)\n\nFinally, notice that we can represent the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of two orthogonal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\underbrace{\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\mathbf{x}}_{\\large \\in S} + \\underbrace{(\\mathbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\mathbf{x})}_{\\large \\in S^\\perp}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Note:** The vector $\\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x},$ whose norm $\\| \\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \\|$ represents the distance from $\\mathbf{x}$ to $S,$ is sometimes called the **vector rejection of $\\mathbf{x}$ from $S.$**" 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186138536 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481665 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186161704 1071 "Workers of a small company decided to elect a new union representative. There are $50$ workers, and $60\\%$ of this worker population will vote for candidate $A$. Let the random variable $X_i$ be equal to\n${1, [math]i[/math]th member of the population will vote for candidateA; 0, otherwise$\nfor $i = 1,\\ldots,50$. Find $P(X_2 = 0 \\mid X_1 = 0)$." 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186161704 1071 "Workers of a small company decided to elect a new union representative. There are $50$ workers, and $60\\%$ of this worker population will vote for candidate $A.$ Let the random variable $X_i$ be equal to\n![](images/c2/c28a8c33f7c9beeb8132564b044489ff480065b047327bda1e8eae8a84218ca2.svg)\nfor $i = 1,\\ldots,50.$ Find $P(X_2 = 0 | X_1 = 0).$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186161704 1074 "We have a population of size $N = 50$, and $p = 0.6$ represents the proportion of the population that will vote for candidate $A$.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The number of members that will vote for candidate $A$ equals\n$Np = 50(0.6) = 30$.\n- The number of members that will *not* vote for candidate $A$ equals\n$N(1 - p) = 50(1 - 0.6) = 20$.\n\nWe select two members of the population at random. We need to find\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 0 \\mid X_1 = 0)\n$$\n\nthe probability that the second member will *not* vote for candidate $A$ given that the first member will *not* vote for candidate $A$ either.\n\nIf the first member does *not* vote for candidate $A$, we have\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\nN - 1 = 50 - 1 = 49\n$$\n\nmembers remaining, and\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\nN(1 - p)-1 = 19\n$$\n\nmembers remaining that will not vote for candidate $A$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 0 \\mid X_1 = 0) = \\dfrac{N(1 - p)-1}{N-1} = \\dfrac{19}{49}\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186161704 1074 "We have a population of size $N = 50,$ and $p = 0.6$ represents the proportion of the population that will vote for candidate $A.$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The number of members that will vote for candidate $A$ equals\n\n$$\nNp = 50(0.6) = 30.\n$$\n\n- The number of members that will *not* vote for candidate $A$ equals\n\n$$\nN(1 - p) = 50(1 - 0.6) = 20.\n$$\n\nWe select two members of the population at random. We need to find $P(X_2 = 0 | X_1 \n= 0),$ the probability that the second member will *not* vote for candidate $A$ given that the first member will *not* vote for candidate $A$ either.\n\nIf the first member does *not* vote for candidate $A,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $N - 1 = 50 - 1 = 49$ members remaining, and\n\n$\\qquad$ $N(1 - p)-1 = 19$ members remaining that will not vote for candidate $A.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 0 | X_1 = 0) = \\dfrac{N(1 - p)-1}{N-1} = \\dfrac{19}{49}.\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186162155 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481664 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186162231 1050 720.0 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186163344 1064 "Suppose that a company is about to elect a new union representative. We're interested in determining the proportion $p$ of a company's employees that will vote for \"candidate $A$.\"\n\nIf the company has $N = 50$ employees, and $30$ of them will vote for candidate $A$, then the proportion $p$ is given by\n\n$$\np = \\dfrac{30}{50} = 0.6\n$$\n\nIn other words, $60\\%$ of the company's employees will vote for candidate $A$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $N$ is the **population size**\n- $p$ is the **population proportion**\n- The number of employees that will vote for candidate $A$ is given by\n$Np = 50\\cdot 0.6 = 30$.\n\nIn this lesson, our goal is to build foundations for estimating $p$ in cases where the population size $N$ is very large. Note that although the population size $N$ is not very large in this example, we can still use it to help develop the key ideas.\n\nWe start by modeling the response from each member of the population as a random variable $X_i$, defined by\n\n${1, [math]i[/math]th member of the population will vote for candidateA; 0, otherwise$\nfor\n\n$$\ni = 1,\\ldots,50\n$$\n\nCalculating the probability that a randomly selected member of the population will vote for candidate $A$ is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X_{i} = 1) &= \\frac{the number of members that will vote for candidateA}{population size} \\\\\n&= \\frac{Np}{N} \\\\\n&= \\frac{30}{50} \\\\\n&= 0.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** It's important to note that the $X_i$'s are *dependent!* For example,\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 1 \\mid X_1 = 1) \\neq P(X_2 = 1)\n$$\n\nTo see why, note that\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 1) = 0.6\n$$\n\nas before. However,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X_{2} = 1 \\mid X_{1} = 1) &= ((the number of members that will vote for candidateA) - 1)/(population size - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{Np - 1}{N - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{50 \\cdot 0.6 - 1}{50 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{30 - 1}{49} \\\\\n&= \\frac{29}{49} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.592 \\\\\n&\\ne P(X_{2} = 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's interpret this result. We know that the first member will vote for candidate $A$. So, to compute the conditional probability that the second member will *also* vote for candidate $A$, we consider a reduced population with $N-1 = 49$ members, of which only $30-1 = 29$ will vote for candidate $A$.\n\nCalculating\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 1 \\mid X_1 = 0)\n$$\n\nworks similarly, as we'll see in the following example." 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186163344 1064 "Suppose that a company is about to elect a new union representative. We're interested in determining the proportion $p$ of a company's employees that will vote for \"candidate $A.$\"\n\nIf the company has $N = 50$ employees, and $30$ of them will vote for candidate $A,$ then the proportion $p$ is given by\n\n$$\np = \\dfrac{30}{50} = 0.6.\n$$\n\nIn other words, $60\\%$ of the company's employees will vote for candidate $A.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $N$ is the **population size**\n\n- $p$ is the **population proportion**\n\n- The number of employees that will vote for candidate $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nNp = 50\\cdot 0.6 = 30.\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, our goal is to build foundations for estimating $p$ in cases where the population size $N$ is very large. Note that although the population size $N$ is not very large in this example, we can still use it to help develop the key ideas.\n\nWe start by modeling the response from each member of the population as a random variable $X_i,$ defined by\n\n![](images/76/76b2ae3dd8206bfdbe208d0e037bd1efa5614101917cf6f981df078cb3cdc8cf.svg)\nfor $i = 1,\\ldots,50.$ \n\nCalculating the probability that a randomly selected member of the population will vote for candidate $A$ is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({X}_{i}=1) & =\\frac{\\text{the number of members that will vote for candidate }A}{\\text{population size}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Np}{N} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{50} \\\\  & =0.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** It's important to note that the $X_i$'s are *dependent!* For example,\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 1 | X_1 = 1) \\neq P(X_2 = 1 ).\n$$\n\nTo see why, note that $P(X_2 = 1) = 0.6,$ as before. However,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1) & =\\frac{(\\text{the number of members that will vote for candidate }A)-1}{\\text{population size}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{Np-1}{N-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{50\\cdot 0.6-1}{50-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{30-1}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{49} \\\\  & ≈0.592 \\\\  & \\ne P({X}_{2}=1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's interpret this result. We know that the first member will vote for candidate $A.$ So, to compute the conditional probability that the second member will *also* vote for candidate $A,$ we consider a reduced population with $N-1 = 49$ members, of which only $30-1 = 29$ will vote for candidate $A.$\n\nCalculating $P(X_2 = 1 | X_1 = 0)$ works similarly, as we'll see in the following example." 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186163345 1064 "Now, suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n$ where some proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let's denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n![](images/32/32bc52b6268f6c5eeb9a6f07fff58c308871cc650541c16a4c595faf81e833df.svg)\nfor $i = 1,\\ldots,n.$ \n\nIt can be shown that the $X_i$'s can be modeled as mutually independent random variables *provided that* the population size $N$ is *significantly larger* than the sample size $n$ (which we denote as $N \\gg n$). In practice, this typically means that the sample size should be no larger than $5\\%$ of the population size.\n\nThe assumption that the population size is much larger than the sample size is true in many cases. For example, the U.S. voting population consists of hundreds of millions of people, yet a typical sample from this population might consist of only a few thousand voters. \n\nLet's now consider the following statistic:\n\n$$\nX = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i\n$$\n\nwhere $X_i \\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p).$ \n\nSince the $X_i$'s take the value $1$ if the $i$th element has the characteristic or $0$ if the $i$th element doesn't have the characteristic, the statistic $X$ can be used to count the number of elements with the characteristic. In other words, $X$ represents the number of sample elements with the characteristic.\n\nWe wish to determine the sampling distribution of $X.$ Note the following:\n\n- Each $X_i$ can be modeled as a Bernoulli random variable, i.e., $X_i\\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p).$\n\n- Since we're assuming that $N\\gg n,$ it follows that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ are (approximately) *independent and identically distributed* (I.I.D) Bernoulli random variables.\n\n- It can be shown that a sum of $n$ I.I.D. Bernoulli random variables with parameter $p$ is a binomial random variable $B(n,p)$ (we'll prove this in a separate lesson). Therefore,\n\n$$\nX = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i \\approx B(n,p).\n$$\n\nWe summarize this process using the following flow chart.\n\n![](images/f5/f50d54432c86d4b41ed842218a70b93617457de3e518aa88cbe6ea28762f37ea.png)\n\nFinally, using the formulas for the mean and variance of a Binomial random variable, we have the following approximations:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p)\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186163345 1064 "Now, suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n$ where some proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let's denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n${1, [math]i[/math]th member of the sample has the characteristic; 0, otherwise$\nfor\n\n$$\ni = 1,\\ldots,n\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that the $X_i$'s can be modeled as mutually independent random variables *provided that* the population size $N$ is *significantly larger* than the sample size $n$ (which we denote as $N \\gg n$). In practice, this typically means that the sample size should be no larger than $5\\%$ of the population size.\n\nThe assumption that the population size is much larger than the sample size is true in many cases. For example, the U.S. voting population consists of hundreds of millions of people, yet a typical sample from this population might consist of only a few thousand voters.\n\nLet's now consider the following statistic:\n\n$$\nX = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i\n$$\n\nwhere $X_i \\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p)$.\n\nSince the $X_i$'s take the value $1$ if the $i$th element has the characteristic or $0$ if the $i$th element doesn't have the characteristic, the statistic $X$ can be used to count the number of elements with the characteristic. In other words, $X$ represents the number of sample elements with the characteristic.\n\nWe wish to determine the sampling distribution of $X$. Note the following:\n\n- Each $X_i$ can be modeled as a Bernoulli random variable, i.e., $X_i\\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p)$.\n- Since we're assuming that $N\\gg n$, it follows that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ are (approximately) *independent and identically distributed* (I.I.D) Bernoulli random variables.\n- It can be shown that a sum of $n$ I.I.D. Bernoulli random variables with parameter $p$ is a binomial random variable $B(n,p)$ (we'll prove this in a separate lesson). Therefore,\n\n$$\nX = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i \\approx B(n,p)\n$$\n\nWe summarize this process using the following flow chart.\n\n![](images/f5/f50d54432c86d4b41ed842218a70b93617457de3e518aa88cbe6ea28762f37ea.png)\n\nFinally, using the formulas for the mean and variance of a Binomial random variable, we have the following approximations:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p)\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186163346 1064 "Suppose we have a population of size $N=1\\,000$ of which the proportion $p=0.5$ has a particular characteristic. We denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n![](images/a8/a801fdb0d37e38011713448593b185b440445d6a57b0ff42e00c21e66401f6bf.svg)\nfor $i = 1,\\ldots,1\\,000.$\n\nTechnically speaking, as we saw previously, the $X_i$'s are *dependent*.\n\nThis time, however, since the population size $N = 1\\,000$ is large, we may *approximate* that the $X_i$'s are *independent*. \n\nTo see why, we first note that \n\n$$\nP(X_2=1) = \\dfrac{Np}{N} = 0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, computing $P(X_2 = 1 | X_1 = 1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1) & =\\frac{Np-1}{N-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,000\\cdot 0.5-1}{1\\,000-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{500-1}{999} \\\\  & =\\frac{499}{999} \\\\  & ≈0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we've shown that\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 1 | X_1 = 1) \\approx P(X_2 = 1),\n$$\n\nsuggesting that $X_1$ and $X_2$ are (approximately) independent." 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186163346 1064 "Suppose we have a population of size $N=1\\,000$ of which the proportion $p=0.5$ has a particular characteristic. We denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n${1, [math]i[/math]th member of the population has the characteristic; 0, otherwise$\nfor\n\n$$\ni = 1,\\ldots,1\\,000\n$$\n\nTechnically speaking, as we saw previously, the $X_i$'s are *dependent*.\n\nThis time, however, since the population size $N = 1\\,000$ is large, we may *approximate* that the $X_i$'s are *independent*.\n\nTo see why, we first note that\n\n$$\nP(X_2=1) = \\dfrac{Np}{N} = 0.5\n$$\n\nNow, computing\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 1 \\mid X_1 = 1)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X_{2} = 1 \\mid X_{1} = 1) &= \\frac{Np - 1}{N - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1000 \\cdot 0.5 - 1}{1000 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{500 - 1}{999} \\\\\n&= \\frac{499}{999} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we've shown that\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 1 \\mid X_1 = 1) \\approx P(X_2 = 1)\n$$\n\nsuggesting that $X_1$ and $X_2$ are (approximately) independent." 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186163347 1064 "Suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n$ where some proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let's denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n![](images/46/4624ca7ee7660803ddc7165252c3725030b13ab255f56dc5d87e23090c2363bf.svg)\nfor $i = 1,\\ldots,n.$ \n\nWe know that if the population sample size $n$ is small compared to the population size $N,$ then the $X_i$'s can be modeled as mutually independent random variables. Therefore,\n\n$$\nX = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i \\approx B(n,p).\n$$\n\nRemember that $X$ represents the number of sample elements with the characteristic. Therefore, we can form an *estimate* for the population proportion $p$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} =  \\dfrac{X}{n} = \\dfrac1 n \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i =  \\overline{X}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p}.$ First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, for the mean of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\text{E}[\\frac{X}{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot \\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot np \\\\  & =p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the variance of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\text{Var}[\\frac{X}{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot np(1-p) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we have the following important results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\n$$\n\nIt should be noted that we don't yet know the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}.$ What we *do* know is that whatever the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is, it has mean $p$ and variance $\\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}.$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186163347 1064 "Suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n$ where some proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let's denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n${1, [math]i[/math]th member of the sample has the characteristic; 0, otherwise$\nfor\n\n$$\ni = 1,\\ldots,n\n$$\n\nWe know that if the population sample size $n$ is small compared to the population size $N$, then the $X_i$'s can be modeled as mutually independent random variables. Therefore,\n\n$$\nX = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i \\approx B(n,p)\n$$\n\nRemember that $X$ represents the number of sample elements with the characteristic. Therefore, we can form an *estimate* for the population proportion $p$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} = \\dfrac{X}{n} = \\dfrac1 n \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i = \\overline{X}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p}$. First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p)\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $\\widehat{\\,p}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[p^(^)] &= E[\\frac{X}{n}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot E[X] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot np \\\\\n&= p\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the variance of $\\widehat{\\,p}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[p^(^)] &= Var[\\frac{X}{n}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n^{2}} \\cdot Var[X] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n^{2}} \\cdot np(1 - p) \\\\\n&= (p(1 - p))/(n)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we have the following important results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\n$$\n\nIt should be noted that we don't yet know the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$. What we *do* know is that whatever the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is, it has mean $p$ and variance\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 false] [17592186163349 1064 "The standard deviation of $\\widehat{\\,p}$  is called the **standard error** of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ and is denoted $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,p}]\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}]} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}}\n$$\n\nIf $p$ is unknown, we can replace it with its estimate $\\widehat{\\,p}$ in the standard error formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\textrm{SE}}[\\widehat{\\,p}]  = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n$$\n\nNote that we have written $\\widehat{\\textrm{SE}}[\\widehat{\\,p}]$ to denote that the above expression is an estimate of $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,p}] .$" 131941