re both perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{100}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{{10}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{r\\cdot r}{10\\cdot 10}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{r}{10}\\cdot \\frac{r}{10}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{r}{10})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{r}{10})}^{2}} & =|\\frac{r}{10}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|r|}{|10|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|r|}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481664 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481653 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481664 1069 "q-162827" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481664 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481664 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=-2$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.16$ | $0.09$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.44$ | $0.21$ | $0.05$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=0,Y=0)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481664 1072 #uuid "34f7a331-3a09-5214-b793-a1233bb14dc6" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481664 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=0)=f(0,0)=0.09.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0)=0.3\\cdot 0.3=0.09.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Checking some possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,-2) & =0.16\\ne 0.3\\cdot 0.6={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-2) & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481665 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481656 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481665 1069 "q-186809" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481665 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481665 1071 "Which of the following number lines represents the inequality $x<-5$ or $x>-3?$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481665 1072 #uuid "4f239d81-ff1a-5084-b54e-f468ceca93a5" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481665 1074 "The inequality $x<-5$ or $x>-3$ means that $x$ can take any value less than $-5,$ or greater than $-3.$\n\nTo represent this on a number line, we graph the two separate inequalities:\n\n- the inequality $x<-5$ consists of an open circle at $-5$ and an arrow to the left.\n\n- the inequality $x>-3$ consists of a open circle at $-3$ and an arrow to the right.\n\nThe resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/21/21bf7706ef220b10f065a8f21c73ea8802740e89f2a5fa5742555f53420899d3.png)" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481666 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481654 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481666 1069 "q-129430" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481666 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481666 1071 "$\\sqrt{{m}^{2}{n}^{2}}=$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481666 1072 #uuid "7244134b-92da-5194-8740-fd44c3d9b173" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481666 1074 "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that ${m}^{2}$ and ${n}^{2}$ are perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{m}^{2}{n}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{m\\cdot m\\cdot n\\cdot n} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{m\\cdot n\\cdot m\\cdot n} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(mn)\\cdot (mn)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(mn)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(mn)^{2}} & =|mn|\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481667 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481661 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481667 1069 "q-40217" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481667 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481667 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal.   Round the answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481667 1072 #uuid "887e0ea0-dd4d-5fcd-a5bc-286204a862b7" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481667 1074 "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,3\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{11}\\sqrt{27}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{33}}{11})≈{58.52}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481668 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481651 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481668 1069 "q-175392" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481668 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481668 1071 "Consider the following predicate:\n\n$\\,$ $P(x,y):$ $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-36>0,$\n\nwhere the universal set is ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following shaded regions represents the truth set of the predicate?" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481668 1072 #uuid "9aa267d9-9214-57a8-8b47-8d7c0275750b" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481668 1074 "The *truth set* of a predicate $P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})$ is the set of $n$-tuples in its universal set $U={U}_{1}\\times {U}_{2}\\times ⋯\\times {U}_{n}$ such that substituting these tuples for the variables makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT=\\{({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\in U\\,|\\,P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is $U={R}^{2}.$\n\nWe need to find $(x,y)\\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\,$ $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-36>0$\n\nLet's first rearrange our inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-36 & >0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2} & >36 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{36} & >1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that this represents an ellipse centered at $O$ with semi-axes $3$ and $2.$\n\nFinally, let's sketch the truth set $T$ of $P(x,y):$\n\n![](images/96/9658f04618246f6c35ab0d22c7998accb5708791cdfc756cd276654b7e8fb18a.png)" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481669 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481662 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481669 1069 "q-40618" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481669 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481669 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal." 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481669 1072 #uuid "a43e3223-e818-53e0-8311-b6df64e15405" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481669 1074 "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\},$ $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\},$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=0[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=0[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{(0)(0)+(-1)(-1)+(0)(2)+(1)(-1)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=0[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Since we got the zero-vector as the projection, the vector $x$ must be  orthogonal to the subspace $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ Thus, the vector $x$ extends the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}.$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481670 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481652 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481670 1069 "q-40617" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481670 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481670 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481670 1072 #uuid "ae3c05b7-f23f-59a0-b648-be66bb1e3e3e" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481670 1074 "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{12\\cdot 5+(-1)\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-1)}{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{12\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-5)+(-2)\\cdot 0}{{2}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The projection is the vector $x$ itself. This means that $x$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$ In other words, $x$ lies in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481671 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481659 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481671 1069 "q-129414" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481671 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481671 1071 "$\\sqrt{{z}^{2}}=$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481671 1072 #uuid "b1106a2d-af4e-5322-aea1-c8e92fa92e1a" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [17592186481671 1074 "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{z}^{2}}=|z|.\n$$" 13194139535224 17592186045417 true] [175921